### Unbuffered Channels
**When to use:** Use unbuffered channels when you need **strong synchronization**. An unbuffered channel guarantees that the sender and receiver have met at the same moment to exchange data (a "rendezvous").

**Key characteristic:** The sender blocks until the receiver is ready, and vice versa.

#### Example: Task Completion Signal
Use an unbuffered channel to ensure a main process doesn't proceed until a background worker has explicitly finished its task.

```go
func main() {
    done := make(chan bool) // Unbuffered

    go func() {
        fmt.Println("Performing heavy work...")
        time.Sleep(time.Second) 
        done <- true // Blocks until main receives
    }()

    <-done // Blocks until worker sends
    fmt.Println("Work finished, proceeding.")
}
```

