### Buffered Channels
**When to use:** Use buffered channels to **decouple the producer and consumer**. They are ideal for smoothing out "bursty" traffic or limiting concurrency.

**Key characteristic:** The sender only blocks when the buffer is full; the receiver only blocks when the buffer is empty.

#### Example: Worker Pool (Job Queue)
Use a buffered channel to hold a queue of pending tasks. This prevents the producer from blocking on every single item, allowing it to queue work faster than the workers can process it (up to the buffer limit).

```go
func worker(id int, jobs <-chan int) {
    for j := range jobs {
        fmt.Printf("worker %d processing job %d\n", id, j)
    }
}

func main() {
    const numJobs = 5
    jobs := make(chan int, numJobs) // Buffered: holds up to 5 jobs

    // Start 3 workers
    for w := 1; w <= 3; w++ {
        go worker(w, jobs)
    }

    // Send jobs without blocking immediately
    for j := 1; j <= numJobs; j++ {
        jobs <- j 
    }
    close(jobs)
}
```

### Summary Comparison

| Feature | Unbuffered | Buffered |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Capacity** | 0 | $> 0$ |
| **Synchronization** | Strong (Synchronous) | Loose (Asynchronous) |
| **Blocking** | Immediate | When full (sender) / empty (receiver) |
| **Primary Use Case** | Signaling / Hand-offs | Queuing / Rate limiting |
| **Risk** | Deadlocks if no receiver exists | Memory bloat if buffer is too large |
